  "worked_solution": "Earlier, we found that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2033}(x) & =-6x-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the points of inflection, we solve ${f}^{\u2033}(x)=0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n-6x-6=0\\,\u27f9\\,x=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-1.$ To do that, we take two points, one to the left of $x=-1$ and another to the right of $x=-1.$ \n\nFor example, let's take the points $x=-2$ and $x=0.$ We compute the sign of ${f}^{\u2033}(x)$ at the selected points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2033}(-2) & =-6(-2)-6=6>0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(0) & =-6(0)-6=-6<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second derivative changes sign at $x=-1.$ \n\nSince the concavity changes at $x=-1,$ we conclude that $x=-1$ is indeed a point of inflection.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
